From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:07:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WOW64: keep a terminated thread's state on iOS while it may
 still run

BTCpuThreadTerm for another thread suspends it, reads its context to
wait for the suspend, and then frees its CPU area and thread state. On
the iOS host the suspend does not always stop the thread: the server
holds a thread only at a safe point and otherwise retries on a timer,
and NtGetContextThread does not wait for that. A busy i386 worker ended
with TerminateThread kept running, met its CPU area pointer cleared in
LockJITContext (a NULL write through TEB+0x1788, ChpeV2CpuAreaInfo), and
kept FEX's lock while that fault was handled, so every thread of the
process spun on it and the childabort probe's terminate phase hung.

On the iOS host a thread terminated by another now leaves the registry
and keeps its state; the server ends it at a safe point in translated
code (madeira-unix 0127). What a terminated thread leaks is its FEX
state. A thread that terminates itself is torn down as before.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-12-fex-thread-faults.md
Offered-upstream: no; the suspend that may not stop a thread is the iOS port's
---
 Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
index b81a94b..0ebf186 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
@@ -830,10 +830,27 @@ void BTCpuThreadTerm(HANDLE Thread, LONG ExitCode) {
     }
 
     Threads.erase(it);
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+    if (StatAllocHandler && Self) {
+#else
     if (StatAllocHandler) {
+#endif
       StatAllocHandler->DeallocateSlot(TLS.ThreadState()->ThreadStats);
     }
   }
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+  /* On iOS the suspend above may not have stopped the thread: the server holds a
+   * thread only at a safe point and otherwise retries later, and NtGetContextThread
+   * does not wait for that. A thread still running would meet its freed CPU area
+   * (a NULL write in LockJITContext) and keep FEX's lock while its fault is handled,
+   * so every thread of the process spins on it (PLA-223). Its state is left to it:
+   * the thread is about to be killed, and the server ends it at a safe point in
+   * translated code. What a terminated thread leaks is its FEX state. */
+  if (!Self) {
+    LogMan::Msg::IFmt("Terminating {}: its FEX state is kept, the thread may still run", ThreadTID);
+    return;
+  }
+#endif
   auto ThreadState = TLS.ThreadState();
 
   TLS.CpuArea() = nullptr;
